It was in Berlin.

87
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:20,640
She happened to be American too, which was just odd because, yeah, I mean, what are the chances?

88
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:24,960
Like someone was, a stranger was approaching and I was thinking like, I can't speak German at the time.

89
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:25,680
I couldn't yet.

90
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:29,120
And I was like, what, how am I going to respond to whatever this woman was going to say to me?

91
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:29,360
Yeah.

92
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,400
But she spoke English and asked me, hey, do you know what time the bus is coming?

93
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:34,160
And I was like, I actually do.

94
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:34,560
Yeah.

95
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:43,440
And then so I started working for Disney and I was there for almost five years designing physical products for babies and toddlers mostly.

96
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:46,000
And that was so fun.

97
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:51,600
I mean, working in a creative industry at one of the most creative, joyful companies on Earth.

98
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,080
And I was definitely a Disney kid growing up.

99
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:57,120
So I just had a blast working there.

100
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:13,200
It had all the wonderful parks you could imagine and also taught me a ton about not only the kids space, but also parents, how they think, what's important to them and what matters when it comes to building a brand from the age of literally zero.

101
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:21,920
And so after Disney, I decided to move back to Canada and I started up here working in marketing.

102
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:31,120
And it was right at that time that my brother Ryan actually, who is the founder of another company based here in Toronto called Snowman.

103
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:37,600
They are the creators of Altos Adventure, Altos Odyssey, a lot of premium indie games that you may know.

104
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:43,600
And yeah, they were about to launch a new video game and I was fresh off the plane from Germany, didn't have a job yet.

105
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:45,520
And he was like, Hey, can you help me?

106
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:48,720
And it was so funny because we used to work together when we were younger.

107
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:52,640
We worked in an educational toy store here in Canada for several years.

108
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:55,200
At the same time, we even had a shared paper route.

109
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:56,640
Then we worked at the same restaurant.

110
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,400
Like, we're very close siblings.

111
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:01,440
And we were like, okay, we shouldn't work together anymore.

112
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:05,920
Like, we don't want work to become our whole lives because, yeah, we're also kind of friends.

113
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:06,640
So, right.

114
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:08,160
You're talking business at Thanksgiving.

115
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:10,000
That's maybe, uh, exactly.

116
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:18,800
Uh, so we were like, this is not a good idea, but I said, like, I'll help you for a few months while I just like re acclimate to Canadian life, look for a job.

117
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:20,000
And he was like, perfect.

118
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:21,760
We just need help for three months.

119
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:22,640
So this is amazing.

120
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:30,000
So I helped them launch what was, um, Altos Odyssey, did like a lot of the marketing and PR for that game.

121
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:33,840
And while I was doing that, I met someone who actually worked for him.

122
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,720
His name's Mateus, and he and his wife, Aster, ended up becoming.

123
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:47,120
And so it was all this kind of like crazy serendipitous journey, but one that led me down a path that I'm absolutely in love with now.

124
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,000
So then how how did Pok Pok start?

125
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:51,600
What was sort of the impetus for that?

126
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:59,600
So Esther and Matthias, their husband and wife, they're actually based in Belgium and they're wonderful people and they have two young kids.

127
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:03,120
At the time they were two years old and freshly born.

128
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:05,280
And they're millennial parents.

129
00:08:05,760 --> 00:08:07,280
They have an affinity for technology.

130
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:08,640
They worked at a game studio.

131
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:12,880
They're both classically trained and award winning animators and artists.

132
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:23,440
When they were starting to look around for something for their two year old to do on an iPad and introduce him to technology in a healthy way, they were really not finding what they wanted.

133
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:33,760
So mostly what was coming up was a lot of overstimulating and more addictive video games that were definitely not designed for young children or designed well for young children.

134
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:38,400
Or they were designed for young children in a maybe not very well way.

135
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:39,920
Exactly.

136
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:52,400
Or very pedagogical apps, you know, centered around learning the ABCs, the one, two, three s, sort of this memorization, regurgitation model, which, you know, has its place in the world.

137
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:59,840
But these kids don't learn best through memorizing and spitting on information, especially under the age of five.

138
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:08,080
And so Esther and her husband were looking around for something that was playbased, something that was open ended, kind of like the toys on the floor of their house.

139
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:09,760
And they were not finding anything.

140
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:15,280
So they started thinking about it and realized like they could, they're artists, like we're talented.

141
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:22,960
We can just tinker something up on our own on the weekends and try to build what their son really loved in real life, which was a busy book.

142
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:32,240
So one of these like look and search books filled with items on a page where there's no text, but you just look around and you can play I Spy, you can guess the sign sounds like just kind of looking.

143
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:33,200
Yeah.

144
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:37,200
And so they started working on that in their free time.

145
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:38,000
It was very simple.

146
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:41,120
You just had some icons and like mostly animals.

147
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:44,800
You tap one, it would make a sound and animate and it was very, very easy.

148
00:09:45,760 --> 00:09:52,160
And right at the time they were kind of conceptualizing this little digital busy book for their little one, they showed it to my brother Ryan.

149
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:54,480
And that was exactly when I was helping with the launch.

150
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:59,520
So we ended up sitting down and talking about it because he was like, Oh, you have to show this to my sister Melissa.

151
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:05,360
She's spent years working in the kids space and I was just lurred by the art style initially.

152
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,280
It was just beautiful.

153
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:08,640
I had never seen anything that had.

154
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:13,120
was designed for children that met them at their level.

155
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:20,800
And what I mean by that is not something that adults think is great for kids, but something that kids actually think is awesome.

156
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:23,520
And the art style was just super minimalist.

157
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:24,800
It only used four colors.

158
00:10:25,680 --> 00:10:28,880
And it was something that was nice enough that I would hang in my own house.

159
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:34,400
And coming from the kids space, there are very few other things that I would do that with.

160
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:39,600
So we got to talk and they told me about the problem they were trying to solve for their kids.

161
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:45,680
And it really resonated a lot with me, you know, speaking of my degree not really having a lot of relevance in my day to day career.

162
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:52,880
There's just so much that we don't learn in school and the education system hasn't changed for the last hundred plus years.

163
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:58,080
And we talked a lot about how our kids are going to grow up to have jobs that haven't been invented yet.

164
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:00,640
And like, how the heck are we supposed to prepare them for that life?

165
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:07,280
And it really came down to thinking about, you know, this is pre CHOT GPT, pre COVID.

166
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:15,360
This is us just thinking like, okay, AI is going to play an important role and a lot of our jobs will be taken over by technology.

167
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:17,840
So what skills do our kids actually need?

168
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,880
And that just came down to creativity, thinking differently, thinking out of the box.

169
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:30,240
And all of that just tied in so beautifully with their interest in building something open ended and child led for their kids.

170
00:11:30,560 --> 00:11:39,040
And so we talked a lot about all these things and ultimately decided that we have to do something about it and that we wanted to do something about it together.

171
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:56,320
So we took this very small idea of a digital busy book and grew it into what became Pok Pok and decided that our mission would be to help raise the next generation of creative thinkers through digital play and technology in a really healthy and mindful way.

172
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:00,800
So okay, so you decided you want to like do something with this, right?

173
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:01,840
This idea.

174
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:08,400
What's the, what's the path from that point to, you know, you've actually released a thing called Pok Pok.

175
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:09,760
I'm curious about that name too.

176
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:13,840
Like, I'm just curious, leading up to that initial release, what were kind of the goals?

177
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:26,080
So our initial plan was to bring the educational piece with together with the play piece from the toys that we all grew up playing with into a digital space.

178
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:33,360
So I hopped in the plane, flew to Belgium, spent a week on Math and Elf Esther's living room floor, and I brought a bunch of toys with me.

179
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:42,400
Obviously, they had a house full of toys, and we just talked and played and played with their kids, and we're thinking about the formative evergreen toys that we used that shaped us.

180
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:46,240
And our initial vision was to do two things.

181
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:54,240
We wanted to push the boundaries of play for toddlers and kids really from two to seven years old using technology.

182
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:58,640
That means inventing toys and kind of putting the toy maker's hat on ourselves.

183
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:13,600
And then the other thing we wanted to do was to take these exceptional evergreen toys that have survived hundreds and hundreds of years in many different forms and bring them into the 21st century and make them better in some way through digitization.

184
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:34,640
So what became POC POC Playroom, our app, is really this collection of ever digital toys that are designed specifically for open-ended child-led play, with no rules, no language, no menus, no limits, and are totally there to spark ideas and questions and creative thinking.

185
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:47,680
And that collection of toys started with six different things, which we launched with in May of 2021, one which was the digital busy book, and has now grown into I think we have over fifteen toys now.

186
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:50,240
So it's evolving pretty quickly.

187
00:13:51,840 --> 00:14:00,640
Was the original idea to have it be a collection of them or at first were you trying to nail it down to one and then you realized there's too many good ideas and it kind of grew from there.

188
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:01,760
Yeah.

189
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:08,800
So we always knew it was going to be more than just one toy because kids are so curious and we knew that we wanted to grow with their curiosity.

190
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:12,320
So we wanted to give them more than just one thing to do.

191
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:17,760
So we wanted to build a collection of toys that would also pique the interest of kids as they grow and change.

192
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:27,680
So thinking about our playrooms growing up, you know, I had a I had Lego, I had puzzles, I had art supplies, I had cars, I had dolls, I had so many things.

193
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:33,680
And some weeks I just really wanted to play with the Lego and other times I wanted to go do something else.

194
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:36,080
And often I actually wanted to mix those all together.

195
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:40,560
And it kind of reminded us of this Montessori toy rotation concept.

196
00:14:41,280 --> 00:14:50,240
And so we thought, what better way to grow with children than to bring the diversity and interest from that real playroom into a digital space?

197
00:14:50,320 --> 00:14:58,000
So we always wanted to build different toys, different kinds of modules to make sure that there was always something interesting and new to explore for kids.

198
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:01,600
Can you go into the is it Montessori?

199
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:11,280
I'm familiar with the word, but it's it's almost like the hierarchy of needs thing where it's like I know the most surface level version of what that is.

200
00:15:11,520 --> 00:15:14,000
Can you explain like what what that is and how it relates?

201
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:15,360
Sure.

202
00:15:15,600 --> 00:15:23,520
So, one of the guiding principles of Montessori is that play is led by the child and play is also open ended.

203
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:42,880
So, for us, we wanted to make sure that we were taking those two principles into POC POC because they are scientifically proven to not only nurture children but actually help develop their brains and not only develop their brains but also build important skills beyond the classroom skills.

204
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:49,360
So, I'm talking life skills like resilience, trust, compassion, empathy, and so on and so forth.

205
00:15:50,240 --> 00:16:08,400
The Toy Rotation is just a typical Montessori concept that you'll find in a Montessori classroom, for example, or a Montessori home, where they, a teacher will take away some toys for a certain period of time and then bring them back later to encourage kids to look at them anew.

206
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:11,280
And honestly, this is something I even used to do with my dog.

207
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:18,240
Like, he would play with the same toy all the time and then suddenly hate it and then just start chewing on my pants.

208
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:23,040
You know, so I would hide the toy for a couple of days and then bring it back and he was like, Oh my God, it's the toy.

209
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:48,000
And so it's like something that actually applies in many areas of life, but with kids., it's a really amazing thing because it, you know, because they're going so quickly as well, when you reintroduce something, sometimes they're looking at it with a completely different brain, you know, like how they play with a spoon when they're three months old versus how they play with a spoon when they're one year old is insanely different.

210
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:50,800
So bringing things in and out can really help with that.

211
00:16:50,960 --> 00:17:00,400
And so we wanted to build that a little bit into POC POC so that kids can hop from toy to to toy and evolve with their interests as they grow.

212
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:05,840
So is that why the, like when you open POC POC after the extremely delightful animation?

213
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:14,080
You get like this kind of collection of toys you can play with, but it's less than you said you're up to fifty now.

214
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:15,600
fifteen.

215
00:17:16,080 --> 00:17:16,640
Oh, fifteen.

216
00:17:16,880 --> 00:17:17,200
Oh, okay.

217
00:17:17,440 --> 00:17:18,080
That makes more sense.

218
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:19,760
So they're all there.

219
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:21,440
They are all there at the beginning.

220
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:22,320
Yes.

221
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:23,600
So they're actually all there.

222
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,520
What we do though is we signify different toys at different times.

223
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:41,920
So for example, we have, um, if we have an update, so let's say we updated our town toy, for example, we'll signify that with a little sort of gentle animation to point kids in that direction just to show them, Ooh, something might be going on here.

224
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:42,160
Yeah.

225
00:17:42,560 --> 00:17:44,640
And not saying, Hey, go play with me right now.

226
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:46,880
But like, oh, something might be going on here.

227
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:50,640
And then a child can decide, do I want to check that out or do I not want to check that out?

228
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:56,880
So that's one way that we, you know, we help guide kids through the experience.

229
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:02,640
And the way that we do this also means that parents don't actually have to be involved if they don't want to.

230
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:16,640
So that sort of became another guiding principle of Pok Pok is when it comes to screen time and our kids, we already feel guilty enough most of the time, even though that's that tends to be a kind of media blitz taboo thing rather than actual science.

231
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:16,960
Yeah.

232
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:25,040
So we wanted to make sure like, okay, if parents are going to be giving their kids some time with Pok Pok and time with the iPod and your iPhone.

233
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:37,280
We would like them to also have the choice of taking a break themselves because parenting is exhausting and sometimes it's nice to have ten minutes to cut up a carrot or go to the bathroom without a kid sitting on your lap.

234
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:45,120
So we made sure that the whole experience was child led and child managed so a parent doesn't actually have to be there.

235
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:53,840
And that's why those little signifiers are really nice because it kind of acts as a hey, why don't you try looking over here for a second and see if that's interesting to you.

236
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:59,520
So instead of having a parent have to say like hey Johnny, have you like why are you always playing with the same thing?

237
00:18:59,680 --> 00:19:00,560
Go check that one out.

238
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:12,480
So it's very subtle, but it's all part of our design ethos, which is to be very gentle and make suggestions rather than force kids to go down a certain path.

