403
00:33:19,600 --> 00:33:29,680
And even if, even if it's not your culture that you are part of or your celebrations that you celebrate, it's so important to show kids what else is out there.

404
00:33:30,480 --> 00:33:33,920
So we like to think of it as like windows and mirrors into the world.

405
00:33:34,000 --> 00:33:42,880
So we want children to have mirrors in POC POC where they can see themselves, their family, their community and connect and feel that appreciation.

406
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:50,800
So if we have a little child who's growing up in a wheelchair and has two dads, we want them to see that exist in POC POC.

407
00:33:51,200 --> 00:34:00,240
But we also want to give kids windows into the world so that they learn about things that they may not see or experience in their daily lives and communities.

408
00:34:00,720 --> 00:34:04,240
So that is why these cultural updates are actually even more important.

409
00:34:04,560 --> 00:34:10,400
I think obviously you want to be able to see yourself in your media and grow up with that validation and support.

410
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:31,520
But I honestly think it's so important crucial for the next generation, especially to see and learn about all different kinds of cultures and people so that it's less scary because I think a lot of the prejudice in the world comes from fear and we're trying in our own very small way to break down those barriers with PACBOC.

411
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:34,160
Yeah, I could not agree more.

412
00:34:34,400 --> 00:34:47,680
I think the simply just, yeah, being exposed to different cultures and seeing them also as, I mean, I feel like like here in the States, we have PBS is our public broadcasting company for TV.

413
00:34:48,640 --> 00:34:50,400
And I still think back today.

414
00:34:51,040 --> 00:34:53,600
on, you know, nineties version of PBS.

415
00:34:54,320 --> 00:35:02,080
They do like a little, you know, five minute segment in between shows on a random holiday or something in a different country.

416
00:35:02,720 --> 00:35:10,960
And then whenever, yeah, you go out into the real world and you meet people from there, like there's at least something there to like connect with at a human level.

417
00:35:11,680 --> 00:35:18,880
And so all those things, even from early childhood, just seeing them represented builds that sort of human connection with other cultures.

418
00:35:20,080 --> 00:35:28,560
It's so great PBS does that too because it's this age range, like under the age of ten, especially where kids form most of their biases.

419
00:35:29,120 --> 00:35:30,400
and assumptions about the world.

420
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:38,720
And if that's only coming from their home, their immediate families and their school, like, it's naturally going to be very limited.

421
00:35:39,200 --> 00:35:55,520
So I think it's our job as anyone who creates content for kids to remember that it's on us to open up the world to them a little bit more because this is that age where they're going to form those biases that could, you know, follow them through their entire lives.

422
00:35:55,840 --> 00:35:57,920
So yeah, we think about that a lot.

423
00:35:58,240 --> 00:36:01,680
And I don't think we've got it perfect to be honest, but we're trying really hard.

424
00:36:02,160 --> 00:36:05,680
Every year, I think we take pretty big steps forward there.

425
00:36:06,080 --> 00:36:08,240
So stay tuned for more, hopefully.

