00:36:22,960 --> 00:36:26,160
Can you go into details on like how you came to that decision?

429
00:36:26,320 --> 00:36:32,560
Like you worked, you know, with a game studio that was paid upfront, at least for most of their games as far as my knowledge.

430
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:35,040
So what made you end up landing on a subscription here?

431
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:38,400
Rass, it just came down to the vision.

432
00:36:38,720 --> 00:36:47,120
So we really wanted to continue to make content for kids over time and not just make one thing, have it exist in the world and kind of walk away from it.

433
00:36:47,520 --> 00:36:52,880
So for us, in order to be able to do that, we need the recurring revenue of a subscription model.

434
00:36:53,200 --> 00:36:59,760
And that also, I think, scratches the edge at the same time for families because kids grow so quickly.

435
00:36:59,840 --> 00:37:10,400
And instead of having to go out and buy a new app every three months or every year, you can subscribe to Pok Pok for literally five, six years and your kid will be able to grow with it.

436
00:37:10,640 --> 00:37:19,360
So I think it it both helps us achieve our mission but also helps families not have to go hunting for other things all the time.

437
00:37:19,680 --> 00:37:21,520
I'm not saying they should only ever use Pok Pok.

438
00:37:21,680 --> 00:37:24,560
Of course, like diversify., that's what's best for children.

439
00:37:25,040 --> 00:37:27,520
But we wanted that piece.

440
00:37:27,680 --> 00:37:40,640
And then on a more practical side, when we when we released the app in 2021, the app, the state of the app store was actually kind of similar to where it is now, but paid apps were not very popular.

441
00:37:41,440 --> 00:37:44,720
It's really hard to grow a paid app from a business point of view.

442
00:37:45,280 --> 00:37:53,280
So for us, making it, making it like, I don't know, a $399 app or something, or a $10 app, even, was never really an option.

443
00:37:53,440 --> 00:38:05,600
We knew like if we wanted to build a business out of this, then it had to be a subscription because freemium or free was just not going to work for us because the way we make pop pop is so unique and arduous.

444
00:38:05,840 --> 00:38:08,240
We needed to, we need to pay our team to do that.

445
00:38:08,720 --> 00:38:14,720
So we couldn't make the app free because we would never dream of advertising to kids ever.

446
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:17,120
So like, yeah, that's true.

447
00:38:17,520 --> 00:38:20,000
There's a whole business model completely locked down.

448
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:20,960
Yeah.

449
00:38:21,520 --> 00:38:24,960
So for us, like, we didn't want any of that in the app.

